<h2>The setting</h2>

<p>The petitioner, a joint venture, had issued a Letter of Intent and Work Order to the supplier before November 2020; the supplier obtained its registration under the MSMED Act, 2006 only thereafter. When payment disputes arose, the supplier approached the Micro and Small Enterprises Facilitation Council, District West, Government of NCT of Delhi under Section 18. Conciliation failed, and the matter moved toward arbitration under Section 18(3). The buyer filed W.P.(C) 12456/2024 contending that the supplier's late registration deprived the Council of jurisdiction to act on the reference at all.</p>

<h2>The Court's reasoning</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Section 8 is discretionary</h3><p>A micro or small enterprise may file a memorandum — the statute grants a discretion, it does not impose registration as a precondition to existence as a supplier.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Section 18 is wide</h3><p>Any party to a dispute under Section 17 may make a reference to the Facilitation Council — language the Court read as keeping the forum open notwithstanding the registration date.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>NBCC applied</h3><p>Following the Supreme Court in NBCC (India) Ltd. v. State of West Bengal, the registration timing objection does not bar the reference itself.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>No prejudice yet</h3><p>Conciliation failed; no claim has been adjudicated; every contention — including jurisdiction — remains open before the arbitral tribunal.</p></div>
</div>

<p>Justice Amit Mahajan's judgment keeps two questions firmly apart. The first is whether the statutory machinery — conciliation, then institutional arbitration under Section 18(3) — can be set in motion by a supplier whose registration post-dates the contract. That, the Court held, it can. The second is what such a supplier can ultimately recover under the Act's delayed-payment provisions for supplies made before registration. That question the Court pointedly did not answer: the impugned orders involved no adjudication upon the rival claims and contentions of the parties, no observations were made on the merits of the claims, and the parties may raise their contentions before the arbitral tribunal. The petition was dismissed.</p>

<h2>Forum now, merits later</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Question</th><th>Where it is decided</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Can the Council receive the reference despite post-contract registration?</td><td>Settled by this judgment — yes; the reference is not barred</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Does the tribunal have jurisdiction over this buyer and this claim?</td><td>Before the arbitral tribunal, under Section 16 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Which supplies attract the MSMED delayed-payment regime, and from when?</td><td>Merits — before the arbitral tribunal on evidence</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Challenge to the eventual award</td><td>Section 34, subject to the 75% pre-deposit under Section 19 MSMED Act</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What buyers and suppliers should take away</h2>

<p>For suppliers, the decision removes a threshold ambush: a Udyam registration obtained mid-relationship does not shut the Samadhaan route, and a buyer's writ petition against the reference is unlikely to succeed where the Council has merely processed the statutory sequence. Suppliers should still expect the registration-timing point to return before the tribunal as a merits defence, and should structure their claims — invoice dates, supply dates, registration date — with that battle in mind.</p>

<p>For buyers, the message is about sequencing and cost. The writ court will not ordinarily intercept a Section 18 reference before the tribunal has ruled on anything; objections must be marshalled before the tribunal, and an adverse award can be challenged under Section 34 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act — but only on depositing seventy-five per cent of the awarded amount as Section 19 of the MSMED Act requires. A premature writ risks dismissal, delay and the hardening of the very proceedings it sought to avoid. The cheaper course is usually to contest the reference on its merits from the first hearing.</p>

<div class="note"><p>The Supreme Court's NBCC (India) Ltd. v. State of West Bengal line of authority distinguishes between the maintainability of a Section 18 reference and the reach of the Act's substantive benefits to supplies pre-dating registration. This judgment applies that distinction at the writ stage for a Delhi Facilitation Council reference; it does not enlarge the substantive entitlements of late-registered suppliers.</p></div>
